# Arbitrary — how a value of a type is assembled.

use std::effects::sample::{ Sample }
use std::nat::{ Nat }
# The arithmetic below needs the Int and Float numeric impls in scope; without
# these, `0 - 100` reports no `NumSub` for Int because only Nat's were imported.
use std::int
use std::float

## A type that can be generated.
##
## The ability says HOW a value is assembled, which is structure — so the
## compiler derives it for records, variants and containers the same way it
## derives `Eq`. Where the numbers come from is not part of any type, so that
## lives in the `Sample` effect instead, and a derived impl performs only
## `<Sample>`: it never mentions `Random`, and the sampler decides whether
## entropy is seeded or live.
##
## ## What this makes possible
##
## A Mezze function takes a single RECORD. So "generate arguments for `f`" is not
## a special mechanism — it is `arbitrary` at `f`'s parameter type, which is an
## ordinary record and derives like any other. That is why `mez sample` needs no
## type-directed code of its own, and why a field whose type has no impl produces
## an ordinary missing-impl diagnostic naming that type rather than a hand-written
## list of what the tool cannot handle.
##
## Property testing is the other consumer, and wants the same generators.
##
## ## Dispatch is on the RETURN type
##
## `arbitrary` takes no arguments, so there is nothing to dispatch on but the type
## being produced. Like a `decode`-style operation, the call site's expected type
## is what selects the impl — which means `arbitrary {}` needs its target pinned,
## and an unannotated `let x = arbitrary {}` is ambiguous rather than wrong.
##
## ## Newtypes must opt in
##
## As with `Embeddable`, and for the same reason plus a stronger one. A newtype is
## opaque, so deriving would pierce that wall — but generation also has a domain
## question the representation cannot answer: `newtype Email = Str` does not want
## arbitrary strings and `newtype Age = Int` does not want negative ones. The
## author opting in is where they say what a valid value IS.
ability Arbitrary for 'a where {
  arbitrary: {} -> 'a <Sample>
}

## The ability operation under a plain name, so callers write `arbitrary {}`.
##
## This was rejected before S-1 with "Annotation declares effects Sample which are
## not used in the implementation" — the op's declared `<Sample>` never reached the
## call site, so the body looked pure and contradicted the annotation. The
## constraint is what carries `'a` out to the caller, whose expected type pins it.
pub let arbitrary: {} with Arbitrary 'a -> 'a <Sample> = {} -> Arbitrary.arbitrary {}

## Spread across zero so a sample shows sign handling, not just positives.
impl Arbitrary for Int where {
  arbitrary = {} -> Sample.int { min = 0 - 100, max = 100 }
}

## Scaled off [0, 1) and shifted, for the same reason.
impl Arbitrary for Float where {
  arbitrary = {} -> (Sample.float {} * 200.0) - 100.0
}

impl Arbitrary for Bool where {
  arbitrary = {} -> Sample.int { min = 0, max = 1 } == 1
}

## NO `Arbitrary for Char` yet, and the reason is a language fact worth recording.
##
## Mezze has no character literal: `'a` is a TYPE VARIABLE, so `'a'` does not
## parse. Every route to a `Char` — `char::from_int`, `char::from_str` — returns an
## `Option`, so a total impl would need a `Char` to fall back to when the option is
## empty, and there is no way to write one. The fallback is circular.
##
## Leaving it out means a record with a `Char` field reports a missing impl, which
## is accurate. Inventing an answer here would be guessing at a language decision.

## The empty string is in the set on purpose: it is the case that breaks parsers,
## and a generator that never produces it is not testing anything.
impl Arbitrary for Str where {
  arbitrary = {} -> do
    let n = Sample.int { min = 0, max = 3 }
    if n == 0 then "" else if n == 1 then "a" else if n == 2 then "sample" else "mezze"
}

## `Nat` is a newtype over `Int`, so it does not derive — the stdlib opts it in,
## exactly as it does for `Embeddable`.
##
## `nat::from_int` is partial, and here the `None` arm is unreachable because the
## range starts at zero. Rather than write a branch that cannot happen, the value
## is built from a non-negative Int through the one conversion that cannot fail.
impl Arbitrary for Nat where {
  arbitrary = {} -> Sample.nat { max = 100n }
}
